{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And Moses sent them, a thousand from each tribe</b> – He did not send the entire army there, even though the Midianites were a numerous people and their cities were very greatly fortified. The reason is that those who had stumbled through the daughters of Moab were many, and they were not worthy to execute the vengeance of HaShem; therefore they chose righteous men, known to their tribes. Now Moses did not command them what to do, but only said to them to inflict HaShem's vengeance upon Midian (Numbers 31:3), and he supposed that they would leave in them neither remnant nor fugitive, like the vengeance upon Amalek or like the vengeance upon the seven nations. And when he saw that they had spared the women and the little ones and the cattle, he was wrathful concerning the women who had known a male by lying with him, for it is fitting for the officers of the host to kill them first of all, both for vengeance and also for the judgment of the Torah — \"and you shall kill the beast\" (Leviticus 20:15). And since he saw that the people were eager to take spoil, he relented concerning the little ones among the women and concerning the spoil. And it is further possible to say that Moses was commanded \"Harass the Midianites\" (Numbers 25:17) and \"Avenge the vengeance of the children of Israel\" (here, verse 2), and he sent there a small force to strike the open cities and to fell every good tree and to stop up every spring of water and to ruin every good field with stones, as is the way of spoilers, and he commanded them nothing except to take vengeance as their hand would find opportunity. And HaShem, blessed be He, to whom belong the wars, delivered Midian and their kings and their cities into their hand. And therefore he was wrathful concerning the women who had known a male by lying with him — not for any other matter — and he commanded concerning the male children for vengeance. Now he was wrathful against the officers of the host but bestowed honor upon Pinchas, for HaShem had given him His covenant of peace. And we have learned in the Sifrei (Matot 43): Pinchas said to him: Just as you commanded us, so we did. And I do not know what this means, for the text does not record that he commanded them anything, and had he commanded them and they did it, how could he be wrathful? And if he commanded them, Heaven forbid that Pinchas should transgress his command, for Saul lost his kingdom on this account (I Samuel 15:11). But the matter is as I have said: he commanded them to inflict HaShem's vengeance upon Midian, and Pinchas said: We have exacted great vengeance upon them. And it is possible to interpret that he said to him: Just as you commanded us from Mount Sinai according to the judgment of the Torah — \"(when you draw near) to a city…\" etc. (Deuteronomy 20:10–18) — so we did to them. And Moses was wrathful concerning the grown women on account of \"these were the ones who caused the children of Israel…\" (Numbers 31:16), and he added every male among the little ones on account of vengeance:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The parenthetical (תצור) appears to be a marginal or interpolated variant of the opening word of Deuteronomy 20:10 (תצור, 'when you besiege'), inserted to indicate the cited verse; translated hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:55.084873612Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}